[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"JavaScript Modules: ES Modules and CommonJS"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"JavaScript modules are reusable pieces of code that can be imported and used in other JavaScript files. They help organize code, improve maintainability, and promote code reuse. There are two primary module systems in JavaScript: ES Modules (ESM) and CommonJS (CJS)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"ES Modules (ESM)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"ES Modules are the official standard module system for JavaScript, introduced in ECMAScript 2015 (ES6). They provide a standardized way to import and export modules in JavaScript."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the 'import' and 'export' keywords."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Supported natively by modern browsers and Node.js (with the '.mjs' extension or 'type': 'module' in package.json)."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"// math.js (ES Module)
export function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

export function subtract(a, b) {
  return a - b;
}"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"// app.js (ES Module)
import { add, subtract } from './math.js';

console.log(add(5, 3)); // Output: 8
console.log(subtract(5, 3)); // Output: 2"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"CommonJS (CJS)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"CommonJS is an older module system primarily used in Node.js environments. It uses the 'require' and 'module.exports' syntax."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Uses 'require' to import modules."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Uses 'module.exports' to export modules."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Primarily used in Node.js."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"// math.js (CommonJS)
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

function subtract(a, b) {
  return a - b;
}

module.exports = {
  add: add,
  subtract: subtract
};"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// app.js (CommonJS)
const math = require('./math.js');

console.log(math.add(5, 3)); // Output: 8
console.log(math.subtract(5, 3)); // Output: 2"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Differences and Considerations"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Syntax: ES Modules use 'import' and 'export', while CommonJS uses 'require' and 'module.exports'."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Asynchronous vs. Synchronous: ES Modules are designed to be asynchronous, allowing for better performance in browsers. CommonJS is synchronous, which is suitable for Node.js environments."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Browser Support: ES Modules are natively supported by modern browsers. CommonJS requires a bundler like Webpack or Browserify to work in browsers."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Node.js Support: Node.js supports both CommonJS and ES Modules. To use ES Modules in Node.js, you can either use the '.mjs' file extension or add '"type": "module"' to your package.json file."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Choosing a Module System"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"For new projects, especially those targeting modern browsers, ES Modules are generally recommended due to their standardized syntax and asynchronous loading capabilities. For Node.js projects, CommonJS is still widely used, but ES Modules are becoming increasingly popular."},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"When working with existing projects, it's important to stick to the module system already in use to avoid compatibility issues. Tools like Webpack and Babel can help bridge the gap between different module systems."}]
